  "query_preview": "..\n\nA well-defined shortwave tracking into western Kentucky/Tennessee \nwill be opening up and absorbed by the northern stream trough \ntracking through the western Great Lakes. Just enough moisture \nadvection and weak forcing will track into the southeastern half of \nthe CWA today, along with enough destabilization (slightly negative \nLI's and SBcapes of 500-750 J/kg) to support the potential for \nisolated showers and possibly a stray thunderstorm. A secondary \nmoisture axis out ahead of a cold front will also track in from the \nwestern Great Lakes late in the day, passing through during the \nevening or washing out altogether. This will provide a secondary \nfocus for showers, but mostly over Lake Huron.\n\nA deep 500 MB low over Hudson Bay on Sunday will direct a strong \nshortwave through the Straights. Timing is now in sync in with the \npeak heating of the day, and scattered showers seem likely north of \nI-69, closest to the mid level cold pool and moisture enhancement \nfrom Saginaw Bay. 850 MB temps dropping into the mid single numbers \nSunday night will bring mins in the 40s as high pressure builds over\nthe region. Low level flow looks to be mostly offshore Sunday night \ninto Monday, but airmass is cold enough to potentially generate some \nlake effect showers which could graze the far eastern Huron and \nSanilac counties before the tail of the upper level PV exits east and\ngood subsidence kicks in. \n\nA strong storm system will track through the northern Plains on \nMonday and into northwest Ontario Monday night. The active warm \nfront is expected to reside over western Great Lakes. Warm and moist \nair will eject out of the southern Plains and fold over into Lower \nMichigan on Tuesday, as PW value reach around 2 inches. With showers \nand embedded thunderstorms likely developing during the day, max \ntemperatures will likely be hindered and capped in the lower 80s. \nThere is still some potential for the timing of FROPA to change \nslightly, however. \n\nMedium range models continue to hang the front near the southern \nMichigan border before returning as a warm front Wednesday night and \nThursday. Euro ensembles offer up a 40-50 percent chance for 24 hour\nqpf to reach or exceed half an inch.",
